A||||1||Johnson's85SE||e-brake cables back in slots||||5||
Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||11-19-2001||11:31 AM||||Hello,<BR> I just changed my rear pads, but ran into a quick problem. I couldn't figure out how to hook in the e-brake cable into the clip on the caliper. I put the spring back on, but the end of the e-brake wire is in the middle of the spring. What is the seceret to puting the cable back onto the caliper?What is the easyist way to compress the spring, when it is in place. Any help appreciated. Thanks, to the forum for walking me though the rear brake pads, (AKA Compressing the blanking piston)<P>------------------<BR>80's Import killer.<BR>[img]C:\My Documents\Stuff\Sports Cars\85fiero.bmp[/img]||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||11-20-2001||12:51 PM||||Usually I can grab the spring with both hands (gloves are recommended), squash it against the bracket and just manage to get the cable end snapped into the E-brake lever on the caliper.<P>One time was having no luck, and had to compress the spring with a long bolt and a washer+nut (the bolt head clamped in a vise on the bench).  I slipped a couple of "zip ties" (those plastic wire bundle restraints w/ a little ratchet in them) through the coils of the spring and pulled them tight, to keep the spring compressed.  Carefully removed the bolt+ nut+ washers, and took the compressed spring over to the car.  Slipped the E-brake cable through the spring, installed the cable, and finally clipped the zip ties off with a diagonal cutters.<P>Even though I was pretty tired, I had sense enough to wear safety goggles and gloves.  If you try it, <B>please</B> do the same.  Compressed springs can hurt you - "They'll put your eye out!" as Mom said.  No kidding.<BR>Be careful, and good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    ||||reg||3
